**Clarify first.** Standard ten-pin rules: 10 frames; each frame has up to two rolls. A strike (10 on the first roll) scores 10 plus the next two rolls. A spare (10 across two rolls) scores 10 plus the next one roll. An open frame scores the pins knocked down. The tenth frame gives one extra roll after a spare and two extra after a strike. Ask: is input a list of rolls or per-frame pairs? Do we score a finished game only, or a partial game? Should invalid input throw?

**Design.** Represent a game as a flat list of rolls; this makes bonuses easy because "the next two rolls" are just the next list elements. Methods: `roll(pins)` validates and records; `score()` walks frames.

```java
int score() {
  int total = 0, i = 0;
  for (int frame = 0; frame < 10; frame++) {
    if (rolls[i] == 10) {                 // strike
      total += 10 + rolls[i+1] + rolls[i+2]; i += 1;
    } else if (rolls[i] + rolls[i+1] == 10) { // spare
      total += 10 + rolls[i+2]; i += 2;
    } else {
      total += rolls[i] + rolls[i+1]; i += 2;
    }
  }
  return total;
}
```
Because the walk stops after ten frames, the extra tenth-frame rolls are only ever used as bonuses and are never double counted.

**Edge cases to test:** all gutter balls (0), all open frames, all spares with a final 5 (150), a perfect game of twelve strikes (300), a strike or spare in the tenth frame, and a game with alternating strikes and spares. Validation: a roll above 10, two rolls in a frame summing above 10, too few or too many rolls, negative values.

**Complexity:** O(rolls), which is at most 21.

**Structure for extension:** keep `Frame` as a small class if the interviewer wants per-frame scores or live partial scoring, and keep validation separate from scoring. Write the tests as you go; the interviewer said many test cases had to pass, so name them after the rule they check.
